1
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,857
Hi, Michelle.

2
00:00:07,857 --> 00:00:08,600
Hello, Harry.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,700
It's nice to see you again.

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:11,400
Hello.

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:13,193
Hi, Susan.

6
00:00:13,193 --> 00:00:15,200
We have both been excited about seeing you

7
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,600
and having lunch with you today.

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,900
Michelle picked these flowers out for you.

9
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,700
Daddy, can we go soon?

10
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,400
We're going to go to lunch in a few minutes, honey.

11
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,400
But I'm thirsty.

12
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:32,380
OK. You go out and get a drink of water at the fountain.

13
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:33,993
The fountain is over there, Michelle.

14
00:00:33,993 --> 00:00:35,399
Near the Exit sign.

15
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:43,200
Thank you.

16
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,903
Michelle is a little shy.

17
00:00:45,903 --> 00:00:49,000
I used to be that way when I was her age.

18
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,100
Harry, if Michelle doesn't want to go,

19
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,200
we don't have to.

20
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,700
She'll be fine.

21
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:55,357
Remember,

22
00:00:55,357 --> 00:00:59,000
I haven't dated any one else since her mother died.

23
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
This is a little difficult for her.

24
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,100
Are you ready to go?

25
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,400
Yes. But could you wait one minute?

26
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,059
I have a call to make.

27
00:01:07,059 --> 00:01:13,500
Would you excuse me?

28
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:15,900
Sam, get Mr. Levine at Toytown Stores

29
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:17,415
on the telephone for me, please.

30
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:24,275
Right.

31
00:01:24,275 --> 00:01:27,186
Hello. Susan Stewart calling Mr. Levine, please.

32
00:01:27,186 --> 00:01:31,200
He's on the phone.

33
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,100
Mr. Levine, Susan Stewart.

34
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,900
I find myself in an embarrassing situation.

35
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,200
I made another lunch date for today

36
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,200
and forgot to enter it in my appointment book.

37
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,590
Can you and I meet for drinks tomorrow?

38
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:51,300
I'd really appreciate it.... Yes.... Thank you....

39
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:54,045
Tomorrow at five o'clock at the Biltmore.

40
00:01:54,045 --> 00:01:54,900
I'll see you then.

41
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:21,700
Thank you, Mr. Levine.

42
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,052
Welcome to the South Street restaurant, folks.

43
00:02:23,052 --> 00:02:23,700
What'll it be?

44
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,051
What do you recommend?

45
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:27,200
Well, the crab salad's always a big hit.

46
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:28,700
Susan, would you like the crab salad?

47
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,400
I'd love the crab salad.

48
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,397
Michelle, would you like to try the crab salad, too?

49
00:02:32,397 --> 00:02:33,600
OK, Daddy.

50
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,300
We'll have three crab salads

51
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,300
and a pitcher of lemonade.

52
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:41,654
Help yourself to celery and carrots and other vegetables.

53
00:02:41,654 --> 00:02:43,700
We used to catch crabs.

54
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:44,200
Where was that?

55
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,400
We had a summer house on Fire Island.

56
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:47,792
Do you remember, Michelle?

57
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:48,300
Sure.

58
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,673
You and Mommy used to take me on the ferryboat.

59
00:02:50,673 --> 00:02:52,187
Sometimes, at night,

60
00:02:52,187 --> 00:02:54,454
we would go down to the beach and catch crabs,

61
00:02:54,454 --> 00:02:55,700
remember?

62
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:57,300
With a piece of meat on a string!

63
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:58,100
Right.

64
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:14,300
Well, I think I'm going to go get us all some vegetables.

65
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:15,977
There you go.

66
00:03:15,977 --> 00:03:24,000
Thank you.

67
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,055
And some ice-cold lemonade.

68
00:03:27,055 --> 00:03:27,728
Thank you.

69
00:03:27,728 --> 00:03:33,700
Enjoy it.

70
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:36,520
Michelle, can I help you with the lemonade?

71
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,183
No, thank you.

72
00:03:37,183 --> 00:03:41,700
I'll wait for my father.

73
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,700
Michelle, can we have a talk?

74
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:45,600
Sure.

75
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,600
I know you miss your mother.

76
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:48,500
You do?

77
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:52,500
Yes. And I'm not trying to take her place.

78
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,200
Then why are you and Daddy

79
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:57,100
spending so much time together?

80
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:58,800
Because we like each other.

81
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:01,900
And right now, he needs a friend.

82
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,100
I'm his friend.

83
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:05,900
I know you are.

84
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:09,100
Sometimes he's very sad.

85
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:11,400
And so are you, I think.

86
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,700
Sometimes.

87
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:17,832
I'd like to be your friend, too.

88
00:04:17,832 --> 00:04:25,911
Will you let me be your friend, Michelle?

89
00:04:25,911 --> 00:04:29,316
So, what were you two talking about?

90
00:04:29,316 --> 00:04:31,100
Just girl talk, Daddy.

91
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,600
It's too hard to explain.

92
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:34,200
You're probably right.

93
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,200
Well, let's get started.
